pinctrl: sunxi: index the bank interrupts by IRQ bank, not by hardware bank

This is what hangs the device when hy310-board-mgr asks for the fan
tachometer interrupt on PH17.

Patch 0004 changed the parent-interrupt lookup from

	pctl->irq[i] = platform_get_irq(pdev, i);

to

	u32 hw_bank = sunxi_irq_hw_bank_num(pctl->desc, i);
	pctl->irq[i] = platform_get_irq(pdev, hw_bank);

which reads the DT "interrupts" list as one entry per *hardware* bank.
It is one entry per *IRQ bank*, in irq_bank_map order. The SoC wires a
GIC line only to the banks that have an EINT block and numbers them
consecutively; it does not leave a gap for a bank that has none.

On the H713 the IRQ banks are PA PB PC PD PF PG PH -- irq_bank_map
{0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7}, which skips 4 (PE, no pins on this SoC). So the
seven lines GIC SPI 54..60 belong to PA..PH, and every bank behind the
gap ends up one line too high with the hw_bank lookup:

	bank      wanted   0004 gives
	PA  (0)   SPI 54   SPI 54    ok
	PB  (1)   SPI 55   SPI 55    ok
	PC  (2)   SPI 56   SPI 56    ok
	PD  (3)   SPI 57   SPI 57    ok
	PF  (5)   SPI 58   SPI 59    = PG's line
	PG  (6)   SPI 59   SPI 60    = PH's line
	PH  (7)   SPI 60   SPI 61    = nothing

That is not a cosmetic mix-up. The parent interrupt is level-triggered
and the chained handler is what clears the source:

	reg = sunxi_irq_status_reg_from_bank(pctl->desc, bank);
	val = readl(pctl->membase + reg);
	if (val) { ...demux, the child's irq_ack clears the bit... }

When PH17 raises its EINT, the PIO asserts SPI 60. The handler sitting
on SPI 60 believes it is bank index 5 = PG and reads PG's status
register at 0x2D4, which is zero. It clears nothing, returns, and the
PIO still holds SPI 60 high -- so the CPU takes the same interrupt
again, forever. CPU 0 never makes progress past the idle loop, RCU
grace periods stop completing and the machine dies. Neither counter in
/proc/interrupts moves: the child (IRQ 230) is never reached, and a
chained parent is not counted at all.

What it is measured against:

* Stock vmlinux (re/vendor/HY310/extracted/vmlinux.elf, ARM32 with
  DWARF). sun50iw12_irq_bank_map at c0e9d894 is {0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7},
  byte for byte ours, and sun50iw12_pinctrl_data at c0e9d814 has
  irq_banks = 7. The vendor's sunxi_pinctrl_init_with_variant does the
  lookup at c0548548:

	c0548548  ldr r8, [r4, #0x90]       ; pctl->irq
	c054854c  mov r1, sb                ; r1 = i, the loop counter
	c0548550  mov r0, r7                ; pdev
	c0548554  bl  #0xc05abd38           ; platform_get_irq
	c0548558  str r0, [r8, sb, lsl #2]  ; pctl->irq[i] = ret

  -- the plain bank index, exactly like upstream. Twenty instructions
  further down, at c0548640..c0548688, the same loop *does* call the
  hw-bank helper (c05475a0) before computing the 0x210 / 0x214 register
  offsets. The vendor distinguishes the two numbers; 0004 merged them.

* The stock fan driver really uses this path: pwm_fan_probe
  (c05d9738) calls gpiod_to_irq at c05d9ff4 and hands the result to
  request_threaded_irq with flags = 3 (both edges) at c05da034. PH17 is
  the only main-PIO EINT user on the stock system -- no vendor DT node
  has interrupt-parent = <&pio>. So the stock kernel takes exactly this
  route with platform_get_irq(pdev, 6) = SPI 60, and it works.

* Upstream does the same and its DTs match: the H616 has irq_bank_map
  {1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8} -- a gap at 4 as well -- and its pio node lists
  seven consecutive lines, GIC SPI 51..57, for those seven banks.

Why it went unnoticed until now: the only GPIO interrupts this tree ever
requested are on r_pio -- the power key on PL4 (patch 0139) and the SDIO
host-wake on PM0. sun50i_h713_r_pinctrl_data has no irq_bank_map at all,
so sunxi_irq_hw_bank_num() returns the index unchanged and both lookups
agree. The bug only bites on the main PIO, only behind the PE gap, and
PH17 is the first pin we have ever asked for there.

The second hunk only replaces a comment that has been wrong since 0140:
irq_read_needs_mux is read by sunxi_pinctrl_gpio_get() alone, the IRQ
request path switches the mux unconditionally now, and the vendor sets
the same flag to 0 (sun50iw12_pinctrl_data word 5).

--- a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c
@@ -1653,15 +1653,26 @@
 		goto gpiochip_error;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * One entry in "interrupts" per IRQ bank, in irq_bank_map order --
+	 * NOT one per hardware bank. The SoC only wires a parent interrupt
+	 * to the banks that have an EINT block, and they are numbered
+	 * consecutively: on the H713 the seven banks PA PB PC PD PF PG PH
+	 * (irq_bank_map 0 1 2 3 5 6 7) are GIC SPI 54..60, with no line for
+	 * the gap at PE. Indexing the DT list by the hardware bank number
+	 * shifts every bank past the gap onto its neighbour's line, and a
+	 * chained handler on the wrong line reads a status register that is
+	 * always zero, never clears the source and live-locks the CPU.
+	 * Only sunxi_irq_hw_bank_num() -- the register offsets -- uses the
+	 * hardware bank number.
+	 */
 	for (i = 0; i < pctl->desc->irq_banks; i++) {
-		u32 hw_bank = sunxi_irq_hw_bank_num(pctl->desc, i);
-
-		pctl->irq[i] = platform_get_irq(pdev, hw_bank);
+		pctl->irq[i] = platform_get_irq(pdev, i);
 		if (pctl->irq[i] < 0) {
 			if (pctl->irq[i] == -ENXIO) {
 				dev_warn(&pdev->dev,
 					 "GPIO IRQ resources missing for bank %u, continuing without IRQ support",
-					 hw_bank);
+					 sunxi_irq_hw_bank_num(pctl->desc, i));
 				pctl->irq = NULL;
 				break;
 			}
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sun50i-h713.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sun50i-h713.c
@@ -724,7 +724,17 @@
 	.npins = ARRAY_SIZE(h713_pins),
 	.irq_banks = ARRAY_SIZE(h713_irq_bank_map),
 	.irq_bank_map = h713_irq_bank_map,
-	.irq_read_needs_mux = false,	/* H713: IRQ works in GPIO mode, mux to func6 kills input */
+	/*
+	 * Only sunxi_pinctrl_gpio_get() looks at this: false means the DAT
+	 * register reflects the pad even while the pin is muxed to the EINT
+	 * function, which is what the vendor driver assumes too
+	 * (sun50iw12_pinctrl_data, stock vmlinux, irq_read_needs_mux = 0).
+	 * The IRQ request path switches the mux unconditionally, see
+	 * sunxi_pinctrl_irq_request_resources() and patch 0140 -- the old
+	 * comment here ("IRQ works in GPIO mode, mux to func6 kills input")
+	 * described the H616 mux value 0x6, not this SoC.
+	 */
+	.irq_read_needs_mux = false,
 	.io_bias_cfg_variant = BIAS_VOLTAGE_PIO_POW_MODE_SEL,
 };
 
